      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.stselectronicrecyclinginc.com/bakersfield-ca#faqpage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What Electronics Can Be Recycled in Bakersfield?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS recycles computers, laptops, phones, servers, printers, copiers, monitors, networking gear, ink, and legacy electronics for Bakersfield businesses. Pickup is free for qualifying commercial volumes across Kern County, and data-bearing devices receive documented sanitization before material recovery. Computer labs at Kern High School District and packing-plant offices at Grimmway Farms typically mix these asset types on one scheduled stop. Servers, storage, printers, and network hardware can be collected with office PCs so Bakersfield teams close a refresh in a single visit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How Does STS Protect Data During Recycling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS Electronic Recycling protects Bakersfield data by applying NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 2 purge methods, then issuing serial-level certificates. Hard drives, SSDs, and mobile devices leave Kern County under chain of custody and are sanitized before any downstream recovery begins. Overwrite, cryptographic erase, degaussing, and physical destruction are available when policy requires them. Under HIPAA 45 CFR §164.312, electronic PHI on disposed devices must be rendered irretrievable, and STS destruction procedures support that standard for Bakersfield clinics such as Adventist Health Bakersfield and Kern Medical."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What certifications do you hold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We document NIST 800-88 compliant sanitization, DoD 5220.22-M methods, and HIPAA-supportive healthcare workflows for IT assets. Bakersfield clients receive chain of custody files and certificates of destruction with each qualifying commercial job. Those records list method and date for data-bearing media so finance and IT can close asset files. Kern County healthcare, energy, and public-sector teams typically keep the certificates in audit packets and vendor due-diligence reviews after a scheduled pickup."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What areas do you serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We serve Bakersfield, Oildale, Rosedale, and the greater Kern County area with scheduled commercial pickup. Organizations searching for electronics recycling near me throughout Bakersfield find STS provides free pickup in Rosedale, Oildale, and all Kern County locations when volumes qualify. Scheduled routes also reach Delano, Wasco, Shafter, and Tehachapi. Downtown Bakersfield, Stockdale, Southwest Bakersfield, and neighboring communities can be included on the same commercial logistics plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is pickup really free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pickup is free for qualifying commercial volumes in Bakersfield and Kern County. A coordinator confirms eligibility during scheduling so your team understands logistics, access needs, and documentation before equipment leaves the dock. Same-week windows are typically available for offices, clinics, plants, and warehouses. Qualifying Bakersfield loads do not require a municipal drop-off, which helps multi-site companies that cannot spare staff for household collection events."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you provide audit documentation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Every qualifying engagement includes chain of custody records plus a certificate of destruction for serialized assets. Accurate reporting helps Bakersfield compliance teams support internal audits, insurer reviews, and vendor due diligence requests. Intake logs list site, date, and asset categories, and gaps in the custody file are flagged before a certificate is issued. Corporate IT Directors in Kern County typically expect serial-level proof before closing a refresh."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you handle large-scale ITAD projects?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Our 200,000 sq ft processing operation handles office refreshes, clinic cleanouts, and multi-site collections across Kern County. Bakersfield organizations can pair electronic waste disposal with data center decommissioning and weekend pickup windows. Energy, agriculture, healthcare, and education sites can stage mixed loads of computers, servers, and networking gear. Coordinators plan dock time around shift changes and security check-in rules so large Bakersfield projects stay on schedule."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens to my equipment after pickup?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Assets collected in Bakersfield leave under tracked custody and travel to our Texas processing operation. Data-bearing media is sanitized to NIST 800-88 purge methods, then remaining materials enter documented recovery streams. Functional equipment may be remarketed after sanitization. You receive certificates and recovery reporting after processing, typically within 48 hours of completed destruction for serialized media, so Kern County teams can retire tagged assets."
          }
        }
      ]
